<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dmitry of Uglich</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1582">1582</a>–<a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Tsarevich Dmitry, Dmitry Ivanovich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dmitry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a> was the youngest son of Ivan the Terrible. After his father's death he lived with his mother at Uglich, where in 1591 he died of a knife wound to the throat. An official inquiry called it an accident during an epileptic fit, but many blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>. Several pretenders later claimed his name, and he was canonised in 1606.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dmitry-of-uglich.jpg" alt="Dmitry of Uglich" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mikhail Nesterov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1899._Tzarevich_Dmitry_by_M._Nesterov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dmitry Ivanovich was born on 29 October 1582, the youngest son of Ivan the Terrible by his last wife, Maria Nagaya, and their only child. Ivan died in 1584 and was succeeded by Dmitry's older brother Feodor I, who was sickly and weak, so a regency council governed the country. From 1586 it was headed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>, Feodor's brother-in-law.</p><p>In 1584 Godunov sent Dmitry, his mother and her brothers into internal exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a>, the tsarevich's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>. Dmitry was the tsarevich, or heir apparent, for close to seven years of Feodor's reign, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> could have contested his legitimacy as heir. He was Ivan's son from his last marriage, which was illegitimate under Russian Orthodox canon law, which allows a maximum of three.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dmitry died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a> on 15 May 1591 in mysterious circumstances. When Feodor died childless in 1598, no other Rurikid heir was left, and Godunov claimed the throne. It was widely believed at the time that he had removed Dmitry to clear the way. Chroniclers and historians offered two scenarios. In the first, Godunov ordered the killing and the assassins made it look like an accident.</p><p>Karamzin, Soloviev and Klyuchevsky supported it, but modern scholarship tends to exonerate Boris. In the second, Dmitry stabbed himself in the throat in an epileptic seizure while playing with a knife, a version held by Pogodin, Platonov, Skrynnikov and others. A third version held that Godunov's men killed someone else and that Dmitry escaped, but most modern Russian historians consider it improbable.</p><p>News of the death caused a riot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a>, stirred by the claims of Maria Nagaya and her brother Mikhail that he had been murdered. The enraged citizens lynched 15 supposed assassins, among them the local government representative and one of Dmitry's playmates. An official investigation led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasili-iv-of-russia">Vasily Shuisky</a> concluded that the tsarevich had died of a self-inflicted wound, and Maria Nagaya was forcibly tonsured as a nun and exiled to a remote convent.</p><p>When political circumstances changed, Shuisky retracted this and said Dmitry had been murdered on Godunov's orders. His remains were moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 3 June 1606 and his cult soon developed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> venerates him as a Saint Pious Tsarevich. A number of impostors claimed his name during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>. Pushkin's play <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>, made into an opera by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modest-mussorgsky">Modest Mussorgsky</a>, presumes the murder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dmitry of Uglich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Uglich later became the scene of the death of <b>Tsarevich Dmitry</b> in 1591.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-uglich">Principality of Uglich</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1587" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dmitry of Uglich"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1587">What was here in 1587? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
